- Total gross income = $75,675

What you can (likely) deduct
1. Student loan interest
- You paid $2,700 to Cornerstone. The student loan interest deduction is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment to income (up to $2,500 for 2023, subject to phaseouts). With your reported income (about $75.7k), you will be well below the phaseout thresholds for married filing jointly, so you can likely claim the maximum $2,500 as an adjustment to income. Keep the loan statement/1098‑E.

2. Charitable contributions
- Your $1,400 to St. Mary’s Food Bank is an itemized deduction (Schedule A). You must keep the receipt/proof of contribution.

3. Unreimbursed expenses ($780)
- Important distinction: If these are expenses related to your consulting (self‑employment) work, they are deductible on Schedule C (business expense). If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (miscellaneous itemized deductions have been suspended). Please confirm whether these $780 are for the consulting activity.

4. Home office (165 sq ft exclusive; home = 850 sq ft; rent $1,600/mo = $19,200/yr)
- The home office deduction is available only if the space is used regularly and exclusively for a business and you are self‑employed (or meet very narrow special categories as an employee — which most employees do not).
- Since you have consulting income, you can claim the home‑office deduction for that self‑employment activity (if the office is used only for that consulting).
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per sq ft × 165 sq ft = $825 for the year.
  b) Regular method: business % of home × allowable home expenses. Percentage = 165 / 850 ≈ 19.41%. Rent $19,200 × 19.41% ≈ $3,726 (this is the home office portion of rent). Under the regular method you’d report the business share of rent and other eligible home expenses on Form 8829.
- Note: The home‑office part is a business expense on Schedule C, not an itemized deduction. It reduces net self‑employment income (and thus both income tax and self‑employment tax). Under Form 8829 rules, the allowable business‑use‑of‑home deduction cannot exceed your gross income from that business less other business expenses — with your numbers the regular method amount (≈$3,726) is less than your consulting gross and other non‑home office business expenses, so it should be fully allowable.

5. Self‑employment tax / Schedule SE
- Your consulting income is subject to self‑employment (SE) tax (Social Security & Medicare). SE tax is computed on the net profit from Schedule C, and one‑half of the SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Schedule 1. The home office and other business expenses lower your net profit and therefore reduce SE tax.

Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- For 2023 the standard deduction for married filing jointly is $27,700.
- Your only itemizable amounts you mentioned are charitable contributions $1,400 (and possibly state/local taxes or mortgage interest if you have them — you did not list any). Even if you added the $1,400 charitable donation to other itemizable expenses you listed, that total is far below the standard deduction.
- Therefore, you should almost certainly take the standard deduction ($27,700) for federal purposes. (Your home‑office deduction is a business expense and does not affect this choice — it reduces business income on Schedule C rather than going on Schedule A.)

Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — reports adjustments to income (student loan interest deduction, half of SE tax, and the Schedule C net profit flows to Schedule 1).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $8,900 consulting, business expenses, and home‑office deduction (or you can use the simplified home office method and still report expenses on Schedule C).
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular method to calculate the home office deduction. If you use the simplified method ($5/sq ft), you do NOT use Form 8829.
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax on net self‑employment earnings.
- Schedule B — if your interest income exceeds $1,500 (it does: $1,975), you must file Schedule B to list interest and dividend sources.
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (unlikely here).
- Keep W‑2 from Desert Sun Logistics and any 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC or other 1099s for consulting and the 1098‑E or loan statement for student loan interest.

State return (Oregon)
