Summary of your income (federal)
- W-2 (Desert Sun Logistics): $64,800
- Self‑employment/consulting: $8,900
- Interest (Citi + E*TRADE): $1,780 + $195 = $1,975
- Total gross income = $75,675

What you can (likely) deduct and how
1. Student loan interest
- Up to $2,500 of student loan interest may be deducted “above the line” (adjustment to income) if you otherwise qualify.
- You reported $2,700 paid; the maximum federal deduction is $2,500 (so you can deduct $2,500 if you meet the eligibility rules).
- You must meet the income/filing-status limits to claim the full $2,500. With the income shown (AGI well under typical phaseout thresholds), you should be eligible for the full $2,500.

2. Charitable contribution
- $1,400 cash gift to St. Mary’s Food Bank is deductible if you itemize; keep the receipt.
- If you take the standard deduction, you cannot deduct this charitable gift on your federal return (there is no universal above‑the‑line charitable deduction for 2023).

3. Unreimbursed expenses ($780)
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses related to a W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended by the TCJA).
- If these are ordinary and necessary business expenses for your consulting (self‑employment) work, they ARE deductible on Schedule C. Please confirm whether they are business expenses for the consulting work.

4. Home office (165 sq ft exclusive use; rent $1,600/month; total home 850 sq ft)
- You appear to qualify for a self‑employed home office deduction (exclusive, regular business use).
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 × 165 sq ft = $825 (flat deduction).
  b) Actual‑expense method: allocate a business percentage = 165 / 850 = 19.41%. Annual rent = $1,600 × 12 = $19,200; rent portion = ~19.41% × $19,200 ≈ $3,731. If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., their business portion would also be deductible and would increase the allowed deduction.
- The actual method (using only rent here) gives ~ $3,730 which is larger than the simplified $825, so the actual method likely yields more tax benefit.
- To use actual expenses you’ll file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and carry the result to Schedule C; simplified method is entered directly on Schedule C.
- Note: home‑office deduction reduces net self‑employment profit (and therefore reduces income tax and self‑employment tax), but keep documentation (floor plan, photos, lease, rent/utility bills).

Other self‑employment effects
- Your consulting profit (Schedule C) will be subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). You pay SE tax on ~92.35% of net profit; rate ~15.3% (half of that is an above‑the‑line deduction).
- There may also be a Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (20% of QBI) for the net self‑employment income — with your relatively small business income this is likely available but is calculated after other adjustments and can be complex.

Standard deduction vs. itemize
- 2023 standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly): $27,700.
- Your itemizable deductions that you reported are: charitable $1,400. Student‑loan interest is not an itemized deduction; it’s an above‑the‑line adjustment. You did not list mortgage interest, large state/local taxes (note SALT cap $10,000), or large medical expenses.
- Given the numbers provided, your itemized deductions are almost certainly far below the $27,700 standard deduction, so you will almost certainly take the standard deduction for 2023.

Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 — your main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments to income (student loan interest deduction; half of SE tax is also entered here).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the consulting income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual expense method for home office. (If you use the simplified method, you do NOT file Form 8829; you enter the simplified deduction on Schedule C.)
- Form 1099‑INT(s) — interest income (these are informational; you report interest on Schedule B if required).
- Form 1099‑NEC (or invoices) — if clients issued them for consulting, these support the Schedule C income.
- Form 1040‑ES — if you need to make estimated tax payments for self‑employment income next year.
- State return: Oregon resident return (Form OR‑40 for 2023). Oregon taxes your income; you’ll report W‑2, self‑employment income, and adjustments on the OR return as required.

Recordkeeping you should keep
- Receipts for charitable gift(s).
- Student loan 1098‑E or other documentation showing interest paid in 2023.
- Documentation that the home office is used exclusively for business (measurements, photos), lease/rent payments, utility bills if you use actual method.
- Business receipts/invoices and records (including the $780 if business‑related).
- Any 1099s or other info statements.

Quick example (rough estimate) — to show impact (illustrative only)
