Quick summary / recommendation
- You’re almost certainly better off taking the 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly ($27,700) unless you have substantial additional itemizable deductions (state/local tax paid, mortgage interest, large medical expenses). Your listed itemizable items (charity $1,400) are far smaller than the standard deduction.
- Your consulting work is self-employment income and belongs on Schedule C; that lets you claim business expenses (including a home-office deduction and the unreimbursed expenses if they are business-related).
- Use Schedule SE to calculate self-employment tax; you get to deduct half of SE tax as an adjustment to income.
- Student loan interest is an above-the-line adjustment (Schedule 1) — limit is $2,500 for 2023 (so you can only deduct $2,500 of the $2,700 you reported, provided your MAGI is below the phase-out range).
- Interest income totaling $1,975 (> $1,500) means you must file Schedule B with your Form 1040.

Detailed walkthrough and calculations (estimates)
Income reported
- W-2 from Desert Sun Logistics: $64,800
- Self-employment consulting: $8,900 (report on Schedule C)
- Interest: Citi savings $1,780 + E*TRADE $195 = $1,975 (report on Schedule B)

Student loan interest
- You reported $2,700 paid. The maximum student loan interest deduction for 2023 is $2,500. So you can deduct up to $2,500 on Schedule 1 (adjustment to income) if your modified adjusted gross income (MAGI) is below the phaseout threshold for MFJ. Based on your income shown, you should be under the phaseout and get the full $2,500 (but I can check more precisely if you want).

Charitable contributions
- Your $1,400 cash gift is an itemized deduction (Schedule A). You have a receipt — keep it. It only helps if your total itemized deductions exceed the standard deduction.

Home office deduction (you have options)
- You have 165 sq ft used exclusively for business, home is 850 sq ft. Percentage = 165 / 850 ≈ 19.41%.
- You pay $1,600/month rent → $19,200/year rent.
  - Regular method: business portion of rent = $19,200 × 19.41% ≈ $3,728. This (plus your allocable share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if any) is deductible on Schedule C. To use the regular method for business-use-of-home you complete Form 8829.
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × 165 sq ft = $825 (no Form 8829; you report the amount on Schedule C). No need to prorate rent or keep utility/bill allocations under simplified.
- Which to pick? Based on rent alone, the regular method (≈ $3,728) gives a larger deduction than the simplified ($825). Run both methods including any utilities you can allocate — the regular method will usually be better here. Note: if you use the regular method and you were a homeowner, depreciation rules would also apply; as a renter you generally don’t depreciate the residence.

Unreimbursed expenses ($780)
- If these $780 are expenses you incurred for the consulting/self-employment work (supplies, travel, phone, etc.), treat them as business expenses on Schedule C and they reduce your self-employment net profit.
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job, they are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended under TCJA).

