Summary answer (short): You’ll almost certainly take the 2023 standard deduction (MFJ = $27,700). Your consulting income is self-employment income and will be reported on Schedule C (and subject to self‑employment tax); the home‑office deduction is available only for the consulting business and can be taken either with the simplified method ($5/sq ft = $825) or the actual method (about $3,727 of your rent alone based on the numbers you gave). Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction but the maximum for 2023 is $2,500 (not $2,700). Charitable gifts are deductible only if you itemize — with $1,400 of charity and no large other itemizable items listed, itemizing won’t beat the $27,700 standard deduction.

Details, numbers, and forms you’ll need

1) Income to report
- W-2 wages from Desert Sun Logistics: $64,800 → reported on Form 1040 using your W‑2.
- Consulting: $8,900 → treated as self‑employment. Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). If your clients issued 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC, include them; even if you didn’t get 1099s, you still must report the income.
- Interest: $1,780 (Citi) + $195 (E*TRADE) = $1,975 → report on Form 1040. Because total interest/ordinary dividends > $1,500, you will also file Schedule B.
- Any brokerage proceeds (if the $195 is investment income beyond simple interest) may generate 1099‑B; report sales/transactions appropriately.

2) Self‑employment tax and related deduction
- Your net profit from consulting (Schedule C) is subject to self‑employment (SE) tax (Social Security + Medicare). You pay SE tax via Schedule SE. You can deduct one‑half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (reported on Schedule 1).

3) Home office
- Eligibility: The space must be used regularly and exclusively for the consulting business and be your principal place of business.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 × 165 sq ft = $825 deduction (no Form 8829).
  b) Actual expense method: allocate home expenses by business use percentage.
     - Business percentage = 165 ÷ 850 ≈ 19.41%.
     - Rent: $1,600/month × 12 = $19,200 × 19.41% ≈ $3,727 deductible as part of Schedule C expenses (plus a proportional share of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., if you have them).
- Which to choose: Actual method often gives a larger deduction here (≈$3,727 from rent alone vs $825 simplified), but actual requires Form 8829 and tracking other home expenses and may introduce depreciation (more complexity and possible later recapture). If you have few other home expenses, actual likely > simplified.
- Note: Home office deduction cannot be for your W‑2 job (employee unreimbursed business expense deductions are suspended for most employees under the current law).

4) Other deductions you mentioned
- Student loan interest: The deduction maximum for 2023 is $2,500 (above‑the‑line). It phases out at higher AGI levels, but with your income (~$75k total before adjustments) you should be well under the phaseout, so you can generally deduct up to $2,500. You must have actually paid the interest in 2023 and receive Form 1098‑E from Cornerstone for interest paid in 2023. (If the $2,700 figure is interest paid in 2023, only $2,500 is deductible.)
- Charitable contributions: $1,400 — only deductible if you itemize (Schedule A). There was no universal charitable “above‑the‑line” deduction for 2023, so if your itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, property taxes/state income taxes up to SALT cap of $10k, medical >7.5% AGI, charitable, etc.) total less than $27,700, take the standard deduction.
- Unreimbursed expenses ($780): If these are business expenses related to your consulting work, deduct them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are not deductible on your federal 2023 return (suspended by TCJA).

5) Quick back‑of‑the‑envelope example (using actual home office for illustration)
- Consulting gross: $8,900
- Business expenses: $780 (other) + $3,727 (home‑office share of rent) = $4,507
- Net self‑employment income ≈ $8,900 − $4,507 = $4,393
- SE tax (approx): 15.3% on 92.35% of net ≈ 0.153 × (0.9235 × 4,393) ≈ $622 ; half SE tax ≈ $311 (deductible on Form 1040)
- AGI rough: wages $64,800 + interest $1,975 + net SE $4,393 = $71,168 → less half SE tax $311 and student loan interest $2,500 → AGI ≈ $68,357
- Subtract standard deduction $27,700 (if you take it) to get taxable income.
(These are illustrative only — exact numbers will depend on all deductible items and exact calculations.)

6) Forms you’ll use (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return).
- Schedule 1 (to report adjustments like student loan interest and the deduction for half SE tax; Schedule 1 also lists other income types).
- Schedule B (if interest/dividends > $1,500).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) for consulting income and business expenses (including home office).
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax).
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual‑expense method for the home office.
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) only if you choose to itemize (likely you will not).
- 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC etc. — gather any you received; 1098‑E for student loan interest; 1099‑INT for bank interest; 1099‑B for brokerage.

7) Oregon state return
- You’ll file an Oregon resident return (Form OR‑40 for residents). Oregon starts from federal AGI and has its own rules; you’ll report the same income amounts, but Oregon may treat certain deductions differently. If you itemize federally or take state adjustments, check OR instructions or consult a pro for state specifics.

8) Documentation to gather
- W‑2 from Desert Sun
- 1099‑NEC / records of consulting income
- 1099‑INT (Citi) and 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV/1099‑B from E*TRADE
- 1098‑E from Cornerstone showing student loan interest paid in 2023
- Receipts for the $1,400 charitable gift
- Receipts/invoices for the $780 business/unreimbursed expenses — and documentation tying them to the consulting work
- Lease/rent receipts and utility bills to support the home‑office actual method

9) Other practical notes
- If the consulting business leaves you owing tax (because SE tax + income tax > withholding), consider estimated tax payments for the current year or increasing withholding to avoid penalties.
- If you expect to continue consulting, keep careful records (mileage logs, receipts) and separate business bank account if possible.
- If you prefer not to deal with depreciation and detailed allocations, the simplified home‑office method is easier but may be smaller.

10) Open questions for you (so I can be more precise)
- Are the $780 unreimbursed expenses for the consulting business or for your W‑2 job?
- Did you actually pay $2,700 in student loan interest in 2023 (and do you have a 1098‑E for 2023)? If the payment(s) were in earlier years, they aren’t deductible on your 2023 return.
- Do you have mortgage interest, property taxes, large state income tax withholding, major medical expenses, or other itemizable items not mentioned?
- Do you want me to run a precise estimate of tax owed/refund under (a) simplified home‑office and (b) actual home‑office scenarios?

